@@ -89,6 +91,43 @@ app.get("/error", (event) => {
 > [!TIP]
 > For enhanced security, H3 hides certain fields of unhandled errors (`data`, `body`, `stack` and `message`) in JSON response.
 
+## Problem Details
+
+Set the `problemDetails` option to render error responses as [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457) problem details:
+
+```js
+const app = new H3({ problemDetails: true });
+
+app.get("/orders/:id", () => {
+  throw new HTTPError({
+    status: 403,
+    statusText: "Forbidden",
+    message: "You cannot access this order",
+    type: "https://example.com/errors/forbidden",
+    instance: "/orders/42",
+  });
+});
+```
+
+The response has `Content-Type: application/problem+json` and this body:
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "https://example.com/errors/forbidden",
+  "title": "Forbidden",
+  "status": 403,
+  "detail": "You cannot access this order",
+  "instance": "/orders/42"
+}
+```
+
+- `type` defaults to `about:blank` when not given. `title` comes from `statusText` and is omitted when it is not set.
+- `data` and `body` are still included. RFC members take precedence over `body` keys with the same name.
+- Unhandled errors use `detail: "HTTPError"`, and their `type`, `instance`, `data` and `body` are hidden.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> When the option is enabled, the error body no longer has the `statusText` and `unhandled` fields. Clients that compare `Content-Type` with `application/json` will not recognize the response. A `headers` value of `content-type` set on `HTTPError` overrides the problem content type. Apps that call `handler.fetch()` without an app do not use this option.
+
 ## Catching Errors
 
 Using global [`onError`](/guide/api/h3#global-hooks) hook:
